 Multimodal Learning in Industrial and Robotic Monitoring
 
-Industrial monitoring covers things like fault detection, checking the physical condition of a system, assessing manufacturing quality and confirming that a robot has completed its task. The traditional approach is to use single sensor readings and engineered features to characterise the system's behaviour. Learned representations take this further by utilising data from images, sound and sensor sequences. Multimodal learning looks at how information from different modalities can be combined to assist with a common prediction task [Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal, Huo2022Multisensor, Thoduka2024Handover].
+Industrial monitoring covers things like fault detection, checking the physical condition of a system, assessing manufacturing quality and confirming that a robot has completed its task. The traditional approach is to use single sensor readings and engineered features to characterise the system's behaviour. Learned representations take this further by utilising data from images, sound and sensor sequences. Multimodal learning looks at how information from different modalities can be combined to assist with a common prediction task \citep{Baltrusaitis2019Multimodal,Huo2022Multisensor,Thoduka2024Handover}.
 
 The literature touches on a number of related areas of research: traditional feature engineering, the use of pre-trained representations, modelling over time, feature combination, prediction fusion and adaptive methods (where the method can adapt to the context). They differ in terms of the information they require, their computational cost, the level of supervision they need and their sensitivity to the quality of the input.
 
-Industrial and robotic monitoring provides a platform on which to compare these approaches as the process can be monitored via a number of different modalities (cameras, acoustic sensors, machine sensors etc). Cameras will obviously provide a view of the visible process, whereas the acoustic and machine sensors provide information which is not visible. The benefit of combining data is highly dependent on the information that each modality retains, the particular prediction that you're trying to make and the test conditions of the system [Wang2020What, Hua2026Weld, Goodarzi2023Deep]. Simply increasing the amount of data does not necessarily lead to better predictions.
